  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework's predictive performance was validated using data from a single port case study in Rotterdam.",
      "what": "This study developed a vessel arrival time prediction framework for inland waterways by integrating port call records, AIS trajectories, and vessel characteristics. Tested on the Port of Rotterdam, the system reconstructs routes to calculate sailing distances and traffic flow, feeding these features into ensemble tree-based models. The model reduced the mean absolute error of arrival predictions by 79.55%, dropping from 17.06 hours in self-reported estimates to 3.49 hours.",
      "why": "It provides a highly accurate prediction method tailored to the structured routes and high traffic density of inland waterways, replacing unreliable self-reported vessel estimates with data-driven predictions that enable proactive berth scheduling."
    }
